\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}

We study how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance in tabletop manipulation. Given a pretrained VLA with vision encoders $\phi_v$ and a language-conditioned action decoder $\pi$, standard fine-tuning minimizes the action prediction loss $\mathcal{L}_\text{action} = \mathcal{L}(\pi(\phi_v(o_t), l), a_t^*)$, where $o_t$ is the visual observation, $l$ is the language instruction, and $a_t^*$ is the demonstration action. We augment this objective with an auxiliary spatial prediction head $h$ that maps intermediate visual features to a spatial target $s_t^*$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda(t) \cdot \mathcal{L}_\text{aux}(h(\phi_v(o_t)), s_t^*)
\label{eq:total_loss}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda(t)$ is a time-varying weight. The spatial target is either a BEV occupancy grid or an egocentric depth map. The auxiliary head is discarded at inference and adds no cost at deployment.

We use OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} % Kim et al., "OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model", arXiv:2406.09246, 2024
with the OFT fine-tuning recipe~\cite{kim2025oft} % Kim et al., "Fine-Tuning Vision-Language-Action Models: Optimizing Speed and Success", arXiv:2502.19645, 2025
as the base model. OpenVLA's vision backbone concatenates patch features from a DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024dinov2} % Oquab et al., "DINOv2: Learning Robust Visual Features without Supervision", TMLR 2024, arXiv:2304.07193
encoder (dimension 1024) and a SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} % Zhai et al., "Sigmoid Loss for Language Image Pre-Training", ICCV 2023, arXiv:2303.15343
encoder (dimension 1152) to form a 2176-dimensional representation per spatial token. The auxiliary heads receive these concatenated features, so their gradients flow through both the spatial (DINOv2) and semantic (SigLIP) encoders simultaneously. We apply LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} % Hu et al., "LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models", ICLR 2022, arXiv:2106.09685
with rank 32, $\alpha = 16$, and no dropout, yielding a conservative effective scaling factor of $\alpha / r = 0.5$.

\subsection{BEV Occupancy Head}
\label{sec:occ_head}

The occupancy head predicts a $64 \times 64$ binary bird's-eye-view occupancy grid from the cached patch features $\mathbf{F} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times N \times 2176}$. The architecture has four stages:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A linear projection from 2176 to 256 dimensions, reshaped to $\mathbb{R}^{B \times 256 \times 16 \times 16}$.
    \item A transposed convolution ($256 \to 128$ channels, kernel size 4, stride 2) with batch normalization and GELU activation, producing $32 \times 32$ spatial resolution.
    \item A second transposed convolution ($128 \to 64$ channels, kernel size 4, stride 2) with batch normalization and GELU, producing $64 \times 64$ resolution.
    \item A $1 \times 1$ convolution ($64 \to 1$ channel) with sigmoid activation, yielding $\hat{y} \in [0, 1]^{B \times 1 \times 64 \times 64}$.
\end{enumerate}
The head contains 1.2M parameters, or 0.016\% of the full 7.65B-parameter model.

Ground-truth occupancy maps are generated offline by replaying recorded demonstration trajectories in MuJoCo and rendering top-down binary grids at each timestep. Each cell is set to 1 if an object body intersects the corresponding workspace region. We train with focal loss~\cite{lin2017focal} % Lin et al., "Focal Loss for Dense Object Detection", ICCV 2017, arXiv:1708.02002
to handle the class imbalance between occupied and free cells:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{occ} = \text{FocalLoss}(\hat{y}, y^*;\; \alpha = 0.25,\; \gamma = 2.0)
\end{equation}
The auxiliary loss weight follows an exponential decay schedule:
\begin{equation}
\lambda_\text{occ}(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp\!\left(-\frac{t}{50000}\right)
\label{eq:occ_decay}
\end{equation}
This schedule progressively reduces the occupancy gradient's contribution so that action prediction dominates in later training stages. The decay can be interpreted as a curriculum: the model first develops spatial structure through the auxiliary signal, then refines motor behavior under the primary objective.

\subsection{Monocular Depth Head}
\label{sec:depth_head}

The depth head shares the occupancy head's architecture with two modifications: the final activation is ReLU (to enforce non-negative depth values) instead of sigmoid, and the training loss is smooth L1:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{depth} = \text{SmoothL1}(\hat{d}, d^*)
\end{equation}
with the same exponential decay schedule ($\lambda_\text{depth}(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp(-t / 50000)$). The depth head has approximately 1.2M parameters.

Ground-truth depth maps are rendered from the egocentric camera viewpoint in MuJoCo at $64 \times 64$ resolution using state replay. Unlike the BEV occupancy grid, which provides an allocentric top-down layout of the workspace, the depth map encodes distances from the robot's camera to scene surfaces. This egocentric frame aligns with the robot's visual observation and directly encodes proximity information relevant to collision avoidance: nearby surfaces produce small depth values, providing a natural gradient for spatial awareness. The BEV frame, by contrast, represents object layout in a coordinate frame orthogonal to the camera view, requiring the encoder to learn a non-trivial viewpoint transformation.

\subsection{Ablation Design}
\label{sec:ablation}

We evaluate five training conditions designed to disentangle the mechanisms by which auxiliary spatial supervision affects collision avoidance.

\textbf{Vanilla.} Standard LoRA fine-tuning with action loss only. Any collision avoidance behavior emerges solely from imitating demonstration trajectories.

\textbf{Full OCC.} LoRA fine-tuning with the BEV occupancy head receiving ground-truth labels. Gradients from $\mathcal{L}_\text{occ}$ flow through both the DINOv2 and SigLIP encoders. This condition tests whether BEV spatial supervision improves collision avoidance.

\textbf{Random OCC.} Identical to Full OCC, but the occupancy labels are replaced with random binary grids that contain no spatial information. Gradient magnitudes and computational costs match Full OCC. Comparing Random OCC to Full OCC isolates the contribution of spatial content: if both perform equally, gradient flow regularization is the dominant mechanism; if Full OCC is superior, the BEV spatial signal provides additional benefit beyond regularization.

\textbf{Detach OCC.} Identical to Full OCC, but gradients are detached at the boundary between the vision encoders and the occupancy head. Only the head's 1.2M parameters receive gradient updates from $\mathcal{L}_\text{occ}$; the encoders see only $\mathcal{L}_\text{action}$. This condition tests whether gradient flow through the encoder is a necessary condition for the auxiliary task's safety effect. If Detach performs worse than Vanilla, the disconnected head acts as a gradient sink that consumes optimization capacity without providing regularization.

\textbf{Depth.} LoRA fine-tuning with the monocular depth head receiving ground-truth egocentric depth maps. This condition compares allocentric (BEV occupancy) and egocentric (camera-frame depth) spatial reference frames while holding the auxiliary training mechanism constant.

We additionally evaluate a curriculum variant (\textbf{OCC-Cutoff}) that applies occupancy supervision for the first 35{,}000 steps then disables it, testing whether late-stage auxiliary gradients interfere with action prediction refinement. This condition separates the effect of early-stage spatial regularization from potential late-stage gradient interference.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ablation design. Each pairwise comparison isolates one factor in the interaction between auxiliary supervision and collision avoidance. The interpretation of the Depth vs.\ Random OCC comparison depends on whether random labels match or underperform real spatial labels (see \S5).}
\label{tab:ablation_design}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Comparison & Isolated Factor \\
\midrule
Full OCC vs.\ Vanilla & Effect of auxiliary spatial supervision \\
Random OCC vs.\ Full OCC & Spatial content vs.\ gradient flow \\
Detach OCC vs.\ Vanilla & Necessity of encoder gradient flow \\
Depth vs.\ Full OCC & Egocentric vs.\ allocentric frame \\
Depth vs.\ Random OCC & Effect of spatial signal fidelity \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This design systematically varies three factors: the presence of gradient flow to the vision encoders, the informational content of the auxiliary labels, and the spatial reference frame. Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design} summarizes the pairwise comparisons and what each isolates.

