\section{Introduction}

Vision-language-action (VLA) models map visual observations and language instructions directly to robot actions, achieving high task success rates on manipulation benchmarks~\cite{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla, kim2025oft}. On SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a 10-task tabletop benchmark with static obstacles in the workspace, a fine-tuned OpenVLA model completes 90.7\% of tasks but achieves only 78.0\% collision avoidance rate (CAR), both measured over 3 seeds and 50 rollouts per task. On the three most physically constrained tasks, collision rates reach 53--93\%. One task yields 100\% task success with only 32.5\% collision avoidance---the robot completes the goal while colliding in two-thirds of episodes. Task success does not imply behavioral safety.

Auxiliary spatial prediction tasks---occupancy grids, depth maps, point clouds---have been widely adopted to improve VLA manipulation performance~\cite{li2026spatialforcing, yuan2025depthvla, liu2025occvla, wu2025spatialvla}. These methods inject 3D structure into vision encoders pretrained on 2D images, consistently improving success rates and generalization. Separately, VLA safety methods apply runtime action filtering via control barrier functions~\cite{hu2025vlsa} or constrained reinforcement learning~\cite{zhang2025safevla} to reduce collisions. These two research threads remain disconnected: no spatial VLA method evaluates collision avoidance, and no VLA safety method exploits spatial representation learning. Whether auxiliary spatial supervision affects collision avoidance---and whether any such effect derives from the spatial information itself or from training dynamics of the auxiliary objective---is an open question.

We present the first factorial study isolating how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance. We train OpenVLA-OFT~\cite{kim2024openvla, kim2025oft} under five conditions that systematically vary gradient flow and label content: Vanilla (action loss only), Full OCC (BEV occupancy with ground-truth labels), Random OCC (random binary labels with matched gradient flow), Detach OCC (ground-truth labels with encoder gradients detached), and Depth (egocentric depth prediction) [PENDING DEPTH RESULTS]. We find that auxiliary gradient flow through the vision encoder is the dominant mechanism behind collision avoidance improvement, while label content plays a secondary role. Conditions with gradient flow---Random OCC (CAR 0.806, 2 seeds) and Full OCC (0.797, 3 seeds)---outperform Vanilla (0.780, 3 seeds), while Detach OCC (0.745, 2 seeds), which blocks encoder gradients, falls 3.5 percentage points below Vanilla. That random labels perform comparably to real occupancy labels (difference of 0.9pp, directionally consistent across seeds but preliminary at current sample sizes) suggests that gradient flow regularization, rather than spatial information, is the primary driver. Our contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Mechanism identification.} We provide evidence that auxiliary gradient flow regularization is the dominant mechanism behind collision avoidance improvements in VLA fine-tuning. Gradient flow is necessary: removing it (Detach OCC) degrades CAR by 3.5pp below the no-auxiliary Vanilla baseline (0.745 vs.\ 0.780, 2 and 3 seeds respectively). Spatial label content is secondary: random labels achieve comparable CAR to ground-truth occupancy labels (0.806 vs.\ 0.797, preliminary at 2--3 seeds per condition).

    \item \textbf{Factorial ablation design.} We introduce a controlled multi-condition study that isolates gradient flow, spatial content, and spatial reference frame as factors in the interaction between auxiliary supervision and collision avoidance. Each pairwise comparison answers one mechanistic question (Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design}), providing a reusable methodology for analyzing auxiliary task effects in VLA training.

    \item \textbf{Practical guideline.} Attaching any auxiliary prediction head to the vision encoder during fine-tuning---even one trained on random labels---improves collision avoidance, provided gradients flow through the encoder. No ground-truth spatial labels are required, and the auxiliary head is discarded after training, adding zero inference cost.
\end{enumerate}

We evaluate on SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa} (10 tasks drawn from LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}) using OpenVLA-OFT with LoRA fine-tuning. Across conditions with gradient flow, CAR improves by 1.7--2.6pp over Vanilla; blocking gradient flow reduces CAR by 3.5pp. Section~2 reviews related work on VLA safety, spatial representations, and multi-task regularization. Section~3 describes the auxiliary head architectures and ablation design. Section~4 presents experimental results and mechanism analysis. Section~5 discusses implications, limitations, and connections to multi-task learning theory.

